          "text": "<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\">\nYou are implementing the PostHandler processing pipeline for a Rust port of the C++ Ledger accounting system. This replaces the current predicate-only Filter system with a proper processing pipeline.\n\n**Project location**: /Users/johnw/src/ledger/rust-port-3/rledger/\n**Build command**: PATH=\"$HOME********/bin:$PATH:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin\" cargo check --workspace\n**Test command**: PATH=\"$HOME********/bin:$PATH:/usr/bin:/usr/local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin\" cargo test --workspace\n\n### Background\n\nThe current filter system uses `Filter<T>` with `matches(&self, item: &T) -> bool` — pure predicate. The C++ ledger uses `post_handler` as a processing pipeline where each handler receives a posting, processes it, and passes to the next handler. Handlers can transform, accumulate, sort, and generate data.\n\n### Task 1: Create `ledger-core/src/handler.rs` (NEW FILE)\n\nFirst, read these files to understand the types you'll be working with:\n- `ledger-core/src/posting.rs` — Posting struct and its fields\n- `ledger-core/src/expr/mod.rs` — Value enum, Expression struct \n- `ledger-core/src/data_structures.rs` — Balance type\n- `ledger-core/src/lib.rs` — module structure\n\nThen create handler.rs with:\n\n**Core trait:**\n```rust\n/// A handler in the posting processing pipeline.\n/// Inspired by C++ ledger's post_handler (Chain of Responsibility pattern).\npub trait PostHandler: Send {\n    /// Process a posting with its parent transaction.\n    fn handle(&mut self, posting: &Posting, transaction: &Transaction);\n    /// Signal end of input (flush buffered state to next handler).\n    fn flush(&mut self);\n    /// Description for debugging.\n    fn description(&self) -> &str;\n}\n```\n\n**Concrete handlers (implement at least these 5):**\n\n1. **CollectHandler** — Terminal handler that collects postings into a Vec for later processing:\n```rust\npub struct CollectHandler {\n    collected: